---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Shot from '../components/Shot.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Every screen — Vora on web, iPhone, iPad, Apple TV, Android and in the car"
  description="Vora on the web, on iPhone and iPad, on Apple TV, on an Android phone, on Android TV and through Android Auto. One library and one account, with an interface shaped for each screen."
>
  <section class="section section--tight">
    <div class="shell">
      <span class="eyebrow">Every screen</span>
      <h1 style="font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.2rem);">One library, every way in</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every client is native to its platform and reshapes itself for the device it launches on — touch
        on a phone, a sidebar on an iPad, the focus engine or a D-pad on the television, and a
        browsable audio tree in the car. Your watch state, watchlist and settings follow you between
        all of them. The iPhone and iPad app is
        <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6836645744" rel="noopener">on the App Store</a>,
        Android is in beta, and Roku is
        <a href="https://channelstore.roku.com/details/243de173df931cd17978bf333f6bfacf:448780000c7862706faff8975d8f24db/vora" rel="noopener">on the Roku Streaming Store</a>.
        Fire TV is out too — <a href="/#get-the-app">grab it from the home page</a>.
      </p>

      <nav class="surface-nav" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;" aria-label="Platforms">
        <a href="#web">Web</a>
        <a href="#iphone">iPhone</a>
        <a href="#ipad">iPad</a>
        <a href="#appletv">Apple TV</a>
        <a href="#phone">Android phone</a>
        <a href="#tv">Android TV</a>
        <a href="#auto">Android Auto</a>
        <a href="#next">Coming next</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="web">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 70ch;">
          <span class="pill">Web</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">The full house</h3>
          <p>
            Any browser on any machine, nothing to install. The web client is also the only place the
            server's administration lives, so this is where you set libraries up — and then mostly stop
            thinking about them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Shot src="web/home" url="https://vora.local" alt="The Vora web client home screen" />

      <div class="gallery gallery--two" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <Shot src="web/library" url="vora.local/library" alt="Library browse on the web" caption="Library" />
        <Shot src="web/discovery" url="vora.local/discover" alt="Discover on the web" caption="Discover" />
        <Shot src="web/live-tv-guide" url="vora.local/live" alt="Live TV guide on the web" caption="Live TV guide" />
        <Shot src="web/music-home" url="vora.local/music" alt="Music on the web" caption="Music" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="phone">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <span class="pill">Android phone</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Native, not a web page in a wrapper</h3>
          <p>
            Kotlin and Jetpack Compose throughout. Video plays through ExoPlayer with picture-in-picture
            and Google Cast; audio keeps going in the background with proper lock-screen controls, a
            sleep timer and an equalizer. Sign in once per server — several servers are fine — and the
            profile picker remembers who you were.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            The Android app is heading to <strong>Google Play</strong> soon.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="phone/home" frame="phone" alt="Vora home screen on an Android phone" />
      </div>

      <div class="gallery gallery--phones" style="margin-top: 3.5rem;">
        <Shot src="phone/library" frame="phone" alt="Library on an Android phone" caption="Library" />
        <Shot src="phone/show-season" frame="phone" alt="A season page on an Android phone" caption="Season" />
        <Shot src="phone/music-player-with-lyrics" frame="phone" alt="Music player with lyrics on an Android phone" caption="Lyrics" />
        <Shot src="phone/podcasts" frame="phone" alt="Podcasts on an Android phone" caption="Podcasts" />
        <Shot src="phone/discovery" frame="phone" alt="Discover on an Android phone" caption="Discover" />
        <Shot src="phone/music-home" frame="phone" alt="Music on an Android phone" caption="Music" />
        <Shot src="phone/internet-radio-player" frame="phone" alt="Internet radio on an Android phone" caption="Radio" />
        <Shot src="phone/release-calendar" frame="phone" alt="Release calendar on an Android phone" caption="Calendar" />
        <Shot src="phone/client-templates" frame="phone" alt="Theme picker on an Android phone" caption="Themes" />
      </div>

      <div style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <Shot
          src="phone/live-tv-guide"
          frame="phone"
          alt="The live TV guide on an Android phone"
          caption="Live guide"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="tv">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 70ch;">
          <span class="pill">Android TV</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">The same app, ten feet back</h3>
          <p>
            Vora notices it is on a television and rebuilds its shell around the remote: an icon rail
            instead of a drawer, a focus ring on everything, and a hero that fills in the details of
            whatever the D-pad is currently sitting on. The live guide becomes a grid you can actually
            steer, and the player controls are built for a remote rather than adapted from touch.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Shot src="tv/home" frame="tv" alt="Vora on Android TV with the focus ring on a poster" />

      <div class="gallery gallery--two" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <Shot src="tv/library" frame="tv" alt="Library on Android TV" caption="Library" />
        <Shot src="tv/live-tv-guide" frame="tv" alt="Live guide on Android TV" caption="Live guide" />
        <Shot src="tv/show-season" frame="tv" alt="A season page on Android TV" caption="Season" />
        <Shot src="tv/music-player-with-lyrics" frame="tv" alt="Music with lyrics on Android TV" caption="Lyrics on the big screen" />
        <Shot src="tv/discovery" frame="tv" alt="Discover on Android TV" caption="Discover" />
        <Shot src="tv/music-home" frame="tv" alt="Music on Android TV" caption="Music" />
        <Shot src="tv/music-player-with-synth" frame="tv" alt="The Synth visualizer and its style menu on Android TV" caption="Synth" />
        <Shot src="tv/music-player-with-lyrics-and-synth" frame="tv" alt="The Synth visualizer above synced lyrics on Android TV" caption="Synth with lyrics" />
        <Shot src="tv/podcasts" frame="tv" alt="Podcasts on Android TV" caption="Podcasts" />
        <Shot src="tv/release-calendar" frame="tv" alt="Release calendar on Android TV" caption="Calendar" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="iphone">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight">
        <div class="spotlight__body">
          <span class="pill">iPhone</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Swift and SwiftUI, all the way down</h3>
          <p>
            A native iPhone app, not a web page in a wrapper. Video plays through AVKit with
            picture-in-picture and AirPlay; music, podcasts and radio share one player that keeps going
            in the background with lock-screen controls, a sleep timer and the Synth visualizer. Sign in
            once per server — several servers are fine — and the profile picker remembers who you were.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            The iPhone and iPad app is out now on the
            <a href="https://apps.apple.com/app/id6836645744" rel="noopener"><strong>App Store</strong></a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Shot src="iphone/home" frame="phone" alt="Vora home screen on an iPhone" />
      </div>

      <div class="gallery gallery--phones" style="margin-top: 3.5rem;">
        <Shot src="iphone/library" frame="phone" alt="Library on an iPhone" caption="Library" />
        <Shot src="iphone/show-season" frame="phone" alt="A season page on an iPhone" caption="Season" />
        <Shot src="iphone/music-player-with-lyrics" frame="phone" alt="Music player with lyrics on an iPhone" caption="Lyrics" />
        <Shot src="iphone/podcasts" frame="phone" alt="Podcasts on an iPhone" caption="Podcasts" />
        <Shot src="iphone/discovery" frame="phone" alt="Discover on an iPhone" caption="Discover" />
        <Shot src="iphone/music-home" frame="phone" alt="Music on an iPhone" caption="Music" />
        <Shot src="iphone/internet-radio-player" frame="phone" alt="Internet radio on an iPhone" caption="Radio" />
        <Shot src="iphone/release-calendar" frame="phone" alt="Release calendar on an iPhone" caption="Calendar" />
        <Shot src="iphone/client-templates" frame="phone" alt="Theme picker on an iPhone" caption="Themes" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="ipad">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 70ch;">
          <span class="pill">iPad</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Made for the bigger canvas</h3>
          <p>
            The iPhone app grows into a sidebar layout on the iPad, in every orientation and at every
            window width. The hero, the guide grid and the player take advantage of the room instead
            of stretching a phone layout across it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Shot src="ipad/home" frame="tablet" alt="Vora home screen on an iPad with the sidebar open" />

      <div class="gallery gallery--two" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <Shot src="ipad/library" frame="tablet" alt="Library on an iPad" caption="Library" />
        <Shot src="ipad/live-tv-guide" frame="tablet" alt="Live guide on an iPad" caption="Live guide" />
        <Shot src="ipad/show-season" frame="tablet" alt="A season page on an iPad" caption="Season" />
        <Shot src="ipad/music-player-with-lyrics" frame="tablet" alt="Music with lyrics on an iPad" caption="Lyrics" />
        <Shot src="ipad/discovery" frame="tablet" alt="Discover on an iPad" caption="Discover" />
        <Shot src="ipad/music-home" frame="tablet" alt="Music on an iPad" caption="Music" />
        <Shot src="ipad/music-player-with-synth" frame="tablet" alt="The Synth visualizer on an iPad" caption="Synth" />
        <Shot src="ipad/music-player-with-lyrics-and-synth" frame="tablet" alt="The Synth visualizer above synced lyrics on an iPad" caption="Synth with lyrics" />
        <Shot src="ipad/podcasts" frame="tablet" alt="Podcasts on an iPad" caption="Podcasts" />
        <Shot src="ipad/release-calendar" frame="tablet" alt="Release calendar on an iPad" caption="Calendar" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="appletv">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 70ch;">
          <span class="pill">Apple TV</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Built for the Siri Remote</h3>
          <p>
            Written in Swift and SwiftUI so it behaves like a tvOS app: the focus engine lifts whatever
            you are on, and the live guide is a grid built for the remote. Music has the same Synth
            visualizer and synced lyrics as the other living room clients.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <Shot src="appletv/home" frame="tv" alt="Vora on Apple TV with focus on a poster" />

      <div class="gallery gallery--two" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        <Shot src="appletv/library" frame="tv" alt="Library on Apple TV" caption="Library" />
        <Shot src="appletv/live-tv-guide" frame="tv" alt="Live guide on Apple TV" caption="Live guide" />
        <Shot src="appletv/show-season" frame="tv" alt="A season page on Apple TV" caption="Season" />
        <Shot src="appletv/music-player-with-lyrics" frame="tv" alt="Music with lyrics on Apple TV" caption="Lyrics on the big screen" />
        <Shot src="appletv/discovery" frame="tv" alt="Discover on Apple TV" caption="Discover" />
        <Shot src="appletv/music-home" frame="tv" alt="Music on Apple TV" caption="Music" />
        <Shot src="appletv/music-player-with-synth" frame="tv" alt="The Synth visualizer and its style menu on Apple TV" caption="Synth" />
        <Shot src="appletv/music-player-with-lyrics-and-synth" frame="tv" alt="The Synth visualizer above synced lyrics on Apple TV" caption="Synth with lyrics" />
        <Shot src="appletv/podcasts" frame="tv" alt="Podcasts on Apple TV" caption="Podcasts" />
        <Shot src="appletv/release-calendar" frame="tv" alt="Release calendar on Apple TV" caption="Calendar" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="surface" id="auto">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="spotlight spotlight--wide">
        <div class="spotlight__body" style="max-width: 70ch;">
          <span class="pill">Android Auto</span>
          <h3 style="margin-top: 1rem;">Your library on the dashboard</h3>
          <p>
            The car gets a real browse tree rather than a mirrored phone screen: Music as Recently Played,
            Top Tracks, Liked Songs, Artists and Albums; Podcasts as new episodes and subscriptions; Radio
            as favourites and all stations. Stations play as a queue, so previous and next skip between
            them, and a star button on the head unit favourites the one you are on — the same star you
            would tap on the phone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Video is deliberately left out. Plays from the car still count, so Recently Played and Top
            Tracks stay honest.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            One caveat worth knowing before you go looking: Android Auto hides media apps that were not
            installed from the Play Store until you enable Developer settings &rarr; &ldquo;Unknown
            sources&rdquo; in the Android Auto app. That is a phone setting, not something the app can
            switch on for you.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="surface" id="next">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Coming next</span>
        <h2>Apple TV is on its way</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Fire TV, Roku, and the iPhone and iPad are already in their stores, and Android is heading to
          Google Play. The Apple TV app shown above is next — native to its platform rather than one
          codebase stretched across all of them, which is the only way a Siri Remote feels right on an
          Apple TV.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="roadmap">
        <article>
          <h3>Apple TV</h3>
          <p>
            Built for the focus engine and the Siri Remote. It is in App Store review now.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p class="note" style="margin-top: 2.5rem; max-width: 74ch;">
        The server side already speaks to it — clients talk to one shared API and pull their look
        from the same design tokens the web and Android clients use — so a new platform is a new front
        end, not a second Vora.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="shell center">
      <h2>Ready to set one up?</h2>
      <p class="lede">One container, one database, your media mounted read-only.</p>
      <div class="btn-row" style="justify-content: center;">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/install">Installation guide</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/features">Back to the features</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
